From 5b2c475910a736622fce2eddf0107a431607f216 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:25:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] The shelf is twenty-nine brands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the 226-product Beauty Market catalogue with 1,841 products from 29
brands, researched from the brands' own storefronts. `--replace` on the
generator, which has argued for appending since it was written; the argument
was right for three partial exports of one retailer and is wrong for a sheet
the owner asked to BE the shelf. What it costs is in the comment: three rounds
of hand-review deleted, and the mass-market tier with it.

Six groups now, and only three are routines. Makeup, Fragrance and Tools are
things you buy, not steps you follow. They stay out of the routine by
ROUTINE_GROUPS rather than by being handed a reading key they have not earned —
365 rows carry no profileKeys at all, which is correct and is why the
"never unrecommendable" guard is now scoped to the three routine groups.

TWO REAL DEFECTS, both found by the new data rather than by review:

  · A retinoid in a CLEANSER was scheduled Morning & Night. The rule that puts
    retinoids at night was scoped to `Serum` and `Moisturiser`, which was true
    of every retinoid the first three sheets held. Photosensitivity is a
    property of the molecule, not the packaging. Now applies to any skincare,
    makeup or body product, and still runs last so vitamin C cannot flip it back.

  · Makeup was being charged to the FACE SKINCARE BUDGET. `categoryOf` sent
    everything that was not hair or body to 'face', safe only while the shelf
    held nothing else. 27 matched lipsticks and foundations were competing with
    cleansers for the same rupees and counting against the cap that stops one
    product eating the routine.

THREE STALE ASSERTIONS, all the same unit bug. The planner budgets in purchase
prices — `cost()` is `p.priceInr` — and three tests compared `monthlyInr`
against a purchase-price budget. They passed because the old shelf's packs were
a month or more of product. A ₹435 hair oil in a 50 ml bottle costs ₹522 a
month, and the units came apart. The caps were never violated.

The floor moved, and in the wrong direction. The cheapest moisturiser matching
an oily profile went ₹47/month to ₹425 because Minimalist, CeraVe, Cetaphil,
The Ordinary, Plum and Biotique left with the old catalogue. A complete face
routine needs ₹900 where it needed ₹700. That is a real regression in what the
shop can offer on a small budget; the numbers moved and the tests stayed, as
BELOW_THE_FLOOR did twice before.

Verified: 16 suites / 177 tests green in src/beauty. The same engine changes
against the OLD catalogue are also 177 green, which is the evidence the six
failures were the shelf and not a regression. Wider API suite, tsc and eslint
unchanged from HEAD.

1,841 of 3,444 catalogued rows are on the shelf. Excluded and counted rather
than fudged: 864 with no verifiable photograph, 550 with no product page to buy
from, 163 with no verified price, 26 timed salon services that are not SKUs.

