From b489a7c771e17e1bf304e5762d5bcc44a22ae698 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 19:23:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] The slider's end goes back above the shelf
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two things the routine page asked for, both measured on the new shelf rather
than assumed.

BUDGET_MAX ₹8,000 → ₹15,000, on the owner's call. The ₹8,000 justification
still standing in this file was measured on a 226-product mass-market shelf
and one of its numbers — "hair at under ₹1,000" — is now wrong by more than an
order of magnitude, since a litre of Olaplex No.4 alone is ₹11,999. Re-measured
across four profiles on 1,259 routine-eligible products: face spends
₹7,635–₹8,122 against the cap with an unbounded ideal of ₹9,505–₹12,447, hair
spends ₹7,614–₹7,823 with an ideal reaching ₹13,938. ₹8,000 had stopped being
headroom and become a ceiling both categories pressed against, so idealInr
fired routinely — "your ideal routine is ₹13,938, your budget is ₹8,000" — on a
slider that could not be moved there.

₹15,000 restores the ORIGINAL principle rather than inventing a new one: the
end sits above every useful maximum the shelf can produce and below the absurd.
The dearest routine the planner will now build is ₹41,903 across 15 products,
and idealInr goes quiet again because the cap no longer binds.

A DERIVED PACK PRICE MAY NOT LEAD A ROUTINE STEP. 230 rows are multi-packs
whose price is arithmetic, not observed — every Remy Laure "x 3" is exactly
0.85 × (3 × single). At the top of the face budget the planner was filling four
of six roles with them, the dearest a ₹21,114 serum 3-pack, so the most
expensive thing the engine would ever tell somebody to buy rested on a number
nobody had seen on a shelf. They stay buyable in the Market and are excluded
from leading a role. Matched on the pack shape in the NAME, not on price or
brand: a dear product is not suspect, and Remy Laure's single-unit prices are
sound and still lead roles freely.

One test broke and its literals were the reason. 'totals what it actually
spent' asserted 24000 (3 × 8000) and clamped from a literal 10000; when the cap
moved, 10000 stopped being above it. Both are BUDGET_MAX now, so the test
checks what it is for — that a total sums what each category was given after
clamping — instead of re-encoding the cap.

Verified: 17 suites / 183 tests green. Across 4 profiles × 3 budgets, 102
planner picks and 143 printed steps: zero from a non-routine group, zero from a
shop-only bundle, zero on the fallback instruction, zero derived-pack prices.

